.TH SNAP 1
.SH NAME
snap \- snapshots of a volume
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snap
.RB [ list
.RI [ path ]]
.br
.B snap take
|
.B delete
|
.B restore
.I name
.RI [ path ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snap
is a command built into the shell,
.BR sh (1).
A snapshot is a read-only picture of a whole volume at one commit: nothing
is copied, so taking one costs a few blocks, at any size. A user's home is
a volume of its own. Each
.I path
(the current directory when there is none) names the volume it is in.
.TP
.B list
The volume's snapshots: the time each was taken (UTC) and its name.
.TP
.BI take " name"
Take one now, named
.IR name .
.TP
.BI delete " name"
Let one go: the blocks only it used become free.
.TP
.BI restore " name"
Make the volume what the snapshot shows (newer snapshots are deleted
first).
.PP
Each volume's root has a hidden
.B .snapshots
directory listing them by name, so
.B ~/.snapshots/before-upgrade/notes.txt
is that file as it was; writing there is refused ("read only"). The file
server also takes one of every volume each hour
.RB ( auto\- date-hour),
keeps them a day, and lets the oldest go first when the disk is full.
.PP
Anyone who may read the volume may list; only its owner (a home's user)
or root may take, delete or restore.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 on failure or a wrong command.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
snap take before-upgrade
snap list
snap restore before-upgrade
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR df (1),
.BR fs (4),
.BR siefs (5)
